import{H as e,_t as t,nt as n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.ByJSXnli.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Design goals and principles","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"contributing/design-goals.md","filePath":"contributing/design-goals.md"}`),a={name:`contributing/design-goals.md`};function o(e,i,a,o,s,c){return t(),n(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[r(`<h1 id="design-goals-and-principles" tabindex="-1">Design goals and principles <a class="header-anchor" href="#design-goals-and-principles" aria-label="Permalink to “Design goals and principles”">​</a></h1><p>This page records the durable product and architectural principles for HeuristicLib. It describes why the library is designed as it is and should outlive the current implementation.</p><p>HeuristicLib is in early alpha. Public APIs may change when a materially stronger design is identified and backward compatibility is not yet a design goal.</p><p>For implementation rules, public API conventions and contributor decisions, see <a href="/contributing/developer-guidelines.html">Developer guidelines</a>. Repository workflow and validation commands live in <code>AGENTS.md</code>.</p><h2 id="north-star" tabindex="-1">North star <a class="header-anchor" href="#north-star" aria-label="Permalink to “North star”">​</a></h2><p>HeuristicLib should become a modern library first toolkit for heuristic optimization that is:</p><ul><li>predictable</li><li>composable</li><li>testable</li><li>easy to embed</li><li>easy to use correctly</li></ul><p>This implies:</p><ul><li>no GUI assumptions</li><li>plain C# composition over framework magic</li><li>explicit execution control and integration points</li><li>a small number of strong concepts instead of many overlapping abstractions</li></ul><p>The library should serve researchers and practitioners first while remaining easy to use from notebooks, services, automation and AI driven workflows.</p><h2 id="core-principles" tabindex="-1">Core principles <a class="header-anchor" href="#core-principles" aria-label="Permalink to “Core principles”">​</a></h2><h3 id="small-coherent-conceptual-model" tabindex="-1">Small coherent conceptual model <a class="header-anchor" href="#small-coherent-conceptual-model" aria-label="Permalink to “Small coherent conceptual model”">​</a></h3><p>The public model should revolve around a deliberately small vocabulary such as:</p><ul><li>problem</li><li>candidate</li><li>search space</li><li>objective</li><li>algorithm</li><li>operator</li><li>run</li><li>search state</li><li>executor</li></ul><p>Prefer one honest concept over parallel hierarchies when their semantics are truly shared.</p><h3 id="composition-and-progressive-api-complexity" tabindex="-1">Composition and progressive API complexity <a class="header-anchor" href="#composition-and-progressive-api-complexity" aria-label="Permalink to “Composition and progressive API complexity”">​</a></h3><p>Algorithms and operators should compose through their ordinary contracts. A composed configuration remains usable wherever its supported contract is accepted, including alongside consumer defined components. Algorithms own optimization orchestration, so replacing or composing components does not require callers to assemble a search loop.</p><p>Common tasks should have short domain entry points over the same machinery available through explicit APIs. Callers can request a final search state, stream progress or retain a run for additional control. Executor construction remains automatic. Additional control should reveal only the responsibilities the caller needs, with predictable ownership, failure and resource sharing semantics.</p><p>Conveniences should compose a small coherent core. Their brevity does not justify redundant execution paths or unnecessary public machinery. Dependencies that affect behavior remain explicit.</p><p>Documentation should introduce the simple path first and link to more control when a task requires it. Learning paths should follow the goals of practitioners, experimenters, integrators and component authors. Custom component authoring and explicit run control are independent choices.</p><h3 id="pit-of-success-and-strong-typing" tabindex="-1">Pit of success and strong typing <a class="header-anchor" href="#pit-of-success-and-strong-typing" aria-label="Permalink to “Pit of success and strong typing”">​</a></h3><p>The library should make correct usage natural and incorrect usage difficult.</p><p>Static typing, explicit invariants and clear API shapes should prevent mistakes where practical. A user should not need deep framework knowledge to configure and run a standard algorithm.</p><h3 id="explicit-dependencies-and-data-flow" tabindex="-1">Explicit dependencies and data flow <a class="header-anchor" href="#explicit-dependencies-and-data-flow" aria-label="Permalink to “Explicit dependencies and data flow”">​</a></h3><p>Behavior affecting inputs such as randomness, cancellation, time, scheduling and caches should remain explicit.</p><p>Avoid ambient state, hidden orchestration and opaque indirection. Execution should be understandable from the configuration and the data passed through it.</p><h3 id="immutable-candidate-flow" tabindex="-1">Immutable candidate flow <a class="header-anchor" href="#immutable-candidate-flow" aria-label="Permalink to “Immutable candidate flow”">​</a></h3><p>Candidates represent values being searched. Existing candidates should remain stable while operators produce new candidates and search states describe public progress.</p><p>This gives algorithms, analysis and concurrent execution a predictable value oriented foundation.</p><h3 id="clear-execution-and-evaluation-boundaries" tabindex="-1">Clear execution and evaluation boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#clear-execution-and-evaluation-boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to “Clear execution and evaluation boundaries”">​</a></h3><p>The problem defines canonical evaluation semantics. An explicit evaluator layer may sit between algorithms and problem evaluation when it provides meaningful composition such as caching, repeated evaluation, observation or scheduling.</p><p>Problems score candidates by returning objective vectors, and evaluators return objective vectors for the candidates they are given.</p><p>Shared operators should support batch oriented APIs when batch context is part of the operation semantics.</p><h3 id="performance-as-a-design-constraint" tabindex="-1">Performance as a design constraint <a class="header-anchor" href="#performance-as-a-design-constraint" aria-label="Permalink to “Performance as a design constraint”">​</a></h3><p>Heuristic optimization invokes the same operator and evaluation paths millions of times in one run. Performance is therefore a first-class constraint on the design itself, not a later optimization pass over a design that ignored it.</p><p>Shared machinery should avoid unnecessary allocation, boxing, delegate creation and interface indirection. An expensive operator does not excuse overhead that is material for a lightweight one, because both travel the same path.</p><p>This constrains abstractions rather than excusing unclear code. An abstraction whose supporting machinery costs more than the behavior it centralizes should not exist. A readable implementation that has not been shown to cost anything should not be replaced by a clever one.</p><p>Where a hot path genuinely needs caller-owned scratch memory, output buffers or caches, express that through overloads or a scoped-state object whose ownership and lifetime rules match the surrounding operator or execution model. Do not invent one-off scratch-memory conventions on isolated operators. Immutable candidates may still allocate when a new candidate is the real result. The goal is to avoid the accidental temporaries around that result.</p>`,38)]])}var s=e(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};